            <div xml:id="d538">

                <pb facs="0606" n="508" sameAs="frus:frus1932v04/pg508"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">893.00/11922: Telegram</note>
                <head><hi rendition="#italic">The Consul at Amoy (Franklin) to the Acting Secretary
                        of State</hi></head>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Amoy</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1932-05-03T16:00:00+08:00">May 3, 1932—4 p.m.</date><lb/>[Received
                        May 3—9:20 a.m.]</dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>For the Minister. Referring to my telegram of May 3, 1 a.m., and to the
                    Minister’s telegram of May 2, 11 a.m. I have this morning at consular body
                    meeting informed my colleagues that my instructions are the same as those
                    received by our senior naval officers and that American naval operations will
                    not be used on International Settlement at Kulangsu beyond those strictly
                    necessary for the protection of American life, in connection with which a menace
                    appears to be present but not in action. At this meeting the consular body
                    recommended to the President of the Municipal Council making efforts to obtain
                    additional police from Shanghai and Hong Kong and issuance of proclamation of
                    assurances that there was no cause for alarm <pb facs="0607" n="509"
                        xml:id="pg509"/> with so many foreign warships in China. Consul Green in
                    Swatow telegraphs me as follows: <quote rend="blockquote">
                        <p>“May 3, 10 a.m. Missionaries who have evacuated Ungkung report that
                            troops sent to combat Communists in Fukien are disbanding and refusing
                            to fight. No Americans at Ungkung, situation being watched closely. So
                            far only slight indication that Communists might enter Eastern
                            Kwangtung.”</p>
                    </quote></p>
                <p>I understand Ungkung is about 10 miles southwest of Chaoan across Fukien border.
                    Repeated to Shanghai and Legation.</p>
                <closer><signed><hi rendition="#small-caps">Franklin</hi></signed></closer>
            </div>
